  "summary": "Four studies directly apply multilevel probabilistic models with partial pooling (Bayesian hierarchical models) to customer churn prediction, making them the primary relevant candidates. [V0209] (El-Sayed et al., 2021) applies this method to the ChurnBench dataset, reporting AUROC of 0.65. [V0212] (Haddad et al., 2024) applies the same method to AgriSat-4, reporting MAE of 0.86. [V0210] (Fischer et al., 2022) applies multilevel probabilistic models with partial pooling to MetroFlow, achieving test F1 of 0.72. [V0211] (Gupta et al., 2023) applies the method to Atlas-ICU, achieving accuracy of 0.79. These four documents satisfy both criteria: correct method (Bayesian hierarchical / multilevel probabilistic with partial pooling) and correct topic (customer attrition/churn). A fifth slot is filled by [V0204] (Fischer et al., 2022), which applies decentralised training to AgriSat-4 for customer attrition (F1=0.64); while not a Bayesian hierarchical model, it is directly applied to the churn topic and ranks above documents that either use the right method on a different topic or use a different method entirely. Documents such as [V0009], [V0225], [V0183], and [V0151] only mention Bayesian hierarchical models for churn as prior work and do not apply them, so they are excluded.",
